-- ============================================================
-- An intro on every category page (SEO PRD, section B): what the topic is,
-- how the guides below are grouped, and where to go next. Categories are
-- not posts, so nothing here touches posts.updated_at.
-- Safe to re-run.
-- ============================================================

update public.categories c set intro_html = v.intro
from (values
  ('resume-format', $i$<p>A resume format is the order and layout of what you put on the page — contact details, summary, skills, experience, projects and education — and the right one depends on who is reading it. A fresher's resume leads with education and projects; an experienced professional's leads with results in their last two roles. Recruiters in India usually spend under a minute on a first read, and most companies run resumes through an applicant tracking system before a person sees them, so a format has to be simple to parse as well as easy to scan.</p><p>The guides below are grouped by who you are: freshers and students, engineering and tech roles, business and finance roles, and other professions, followed by section-by-section help. Each one has a sample you can copy, what to put in every section for that role, and the mistakes that get resumes rejected. When you have picked a format, check it with the free <a href="/tools/resume-ats-checker">resume ATS checker</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('interview-prep', $i$<p>Most interviews in Indian hiring follow the same shape: an online assessment or aptitude round, one or two technical rounds, and an HR round. What changes from company to company is the weight of each — a service company's technical round tests fundamentals and a little coding, while a product company goes deep on data structures and problem solving. Knowing which round you are facing is half the preparation.</p><p>The guides below are grouped so you can prepare for the interview in front of you: company-wise guides for TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Cognizant, Accenture, Capgemini, Deloitte, Amazon and others; technical questions by subject; the HR and behavioural questions every interviewer asks; and the skills of interviewing itself — group discussions, video interviews and following up.</p>$i$),
  ('salary', $i$<p>The number on your offer letter is your CTC — cost to company — and it is not what reaches your bank account. Employer PF, gratuity, variable pay and insurance are counted inside CTC, and then employee PF, professional tax and income tax come out of what is left. That is why two offers with the same CTC can pay very different amounts each month.</p><p>Start with the in-hand salary guides, one for every common package from 3 LPA to 1 crore, each with a month-by-month breakdown under both tax regimes. Or put your own numbers into the free <a href="/tools/in-hand-salary-calculator">in-hand salary calculator</a>. The rest of this section covers negotiation — what to say when asked for your expected CTC, how much of a hike to ask for when switching, and how to write a negotiation email. Deductions themselves are explained in <a href="/blog/category/pf-tax">PF, Tax &amp; CTC</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('pf-tax', $i$<p>Every salaried employee in India pays into the Employees' Provident Fund, may become eligible for gratuity, and has to choose between the old and new income tax regimes — usually without anyone explaining how any of it works. This section is that explanation, written against the current rules and with worked numbers.</p><p>The guides are grouped by topic. EPF and EPS covers checking your balance, UAN and KYC, withdrawals and advances, transferring PF when you change jobs, nomination, the interest rate and how your pension is calculated. Tax covers Form 16, filing your ITR, HRA exemption, the standard deduction and choosing a regime. CTC and payslip explains basic pay, variable pay and every line on a salary slip, and the last group covers gratuity and leave encashment. To see what all of this does to your monthly pay, use the <a href="/tools/in-hand-salary-calculator">in-hand salary calculator</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('letters-formats', $i$<p>Some documents come up at every stage of a job: a leave application while you study, an internship certificate and a bonafide letter, then an offer letter, payslips, a resignation letter and finally relieving and experience letters when you leave. Each has a standard shape that HR teams in India expect, and a missing line — a last working day, a designation, a signature — is often the reason one gets sent back.</p><p>Every guide in this section gives you a format to copy, filled samples for common situations, and a note on what each part is for and what to check before you send or sign it. There are also short templates for the emails around a job search — following up on an application and asking for a referral. For the cover letter that goes with an application, see <a href="/blog/category/cover-letter">Cover Letters</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('career-growth', $i$<p>After the first job, the questions change. How long should you stay? What appraisal hike is normal, and how do you ask for more? Is a counter-offer worth taking? What does your notice period actually oblige you to do, and can it be bought out? And if the work you are doing is not the work you want, how do you switch careers without starting from zero?</p><p>The guides in this section answer those questions for people in their first few years of work in India: raises and appraisals, notice periods and probation, deciding when and how to switch jobs, choosing between a startup, an MNC, a service company and a product company, and moving into IT, data analytics or product management from another field. When you are ready to move, <a href="/blog/category/resume-format">update your resume</a> and see what the switch should pay with the <a href="/tools/in-hand-salary-calculator">salary calculator</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('job-search', $i$<p>Finding your first job in India is a numbers game played with very little information: which portals actually have openings, which cities hire freshers in your field, whether to wait for campus placement or apply off-campus, and what to do when the CGPA cut-off rules you out. This section is the practical mechanics of all of it.</p><p>You will find guides to job portals and Naukri, city-wise lists of IT companies hiring freshers, campus placement and pre-placement offers, internships — paid, virtual and summer — remote and part-time work, and the parts of the process nobody explains, such as background verification and probation. If you are applying now, pair these with a resume from <a href="/blog/category/resume-format">Resume Formats</a> and run it through the <a href="/tools/resume-ats-checker">ATS checker</a> first.</p>$i$),
  ('ats-resume', $i$<p>An applicant tracking system is the software most companies use to collect applications and filter them before a recruiter reads anything. It turns your resume into text, pulls out sections like experience and skills, and ranks you against the job's keywords. A resume that looks good to a person can still come out of that process scrambled — two columns, text inside images, headers it does not recognise.</p><p>The guides in this section explain how these systems read a resume and what makes one parse cleanly: layouts and file types, section headings, keywords taken from the job description, and how to tailor one resume to many applications without rewriting it each time. To see what an ATS reads from your own file, upload it to the free <a href="/tools/resume-ats-checker">resume ATS checker</a> — it runs in your browser and shows a score with the exact lines to fix.</p>$i$),
  ('resume-builder', $i$<p>A resume builder saves you from fighting a word processor, but not every builder produces a file that an applicant tracking system can read. Some export resumes as images, some lock the best templates behind a payment at the download step, and some produce two-column layouts that look sharp and parse badly.</p><p>The guides in this section compare resume builders and CV makers available in India, explain what to look for before you spend time filling one in, and show how to get a clean, ATS-friendly PDF out of whichever you use. If you would rather start now, Cheatcode's own resume builder has free ATS-friendly templates, and you can check any resume you already have with the <a href="/tools/resume-ats-checker">resume ATS checker</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('cover-letter', $i$<p>Many applications in India no longer ask for a cover letter, but the ones that do — internships, smaller companies, roles where you are changing fields, and any job applied to by email — read it, and a good one is often what gets a borderline resume a call. It does not need to be long: three short paragraphs that say what you are applying for, why you fit, and what you would like to happen next.</p><p>The guides in this section give you formats and examples for freshers, internships and experienced applicants, plus how to write a cover letter in the body of an email. For other documents you will need during a job — offer, resignation and relieving letters — see <a href="/blog/category/letters-formats">Letters &amp; Formats</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('ai-job-search', $i$<p>AI tools now promise to write your resume, match you to jobs and apply on your behalf. Some save hours; some send the same generic application to hundreds of companies and get your profile ignored. Knowing which is which matters, because recruiters notice volume without fit.</p><p>The guides in this section look at auto-apply tools, AI job matching and AI resume writers available to job seekers in India — what they do well, where they hurt you, and how to use them to apply faster to the right jobs rather than more of the wrong ones. They also cover how employers use AI to screen candidates, so you know what is reading your application. Whatever you use, check the resume it produces with the <a href="/tools/resume-ats-checker">ATS checker</a> before you send it.</p>$i$),
  ('mentorship', $i$<p>A good mentor is someone a few years ahead of you in the career you want, who can tell you what actually matters in it — which skills, which companies, which mistakes to avoid. Advice like that is hard to find from a classroom or a search engine, and it is the shortcut most successful early-career professionals had.</p><p>The guides in this section cover how to find a mentor and what to ask one, when a paid career coach is worth it, and the big early decisions where a second opinion helps most: becoming a data analyst or software engineer, an MBA after engineering, or a master's abroad against a job in India. For decisions after your first job — raises, notice periods and switching careers — see <a href="/blog/category/career-growth">Career Growth</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('linkedin', $i$<p>Recruiters in India search LinkedIn every day, and most profiles never show up in those searches because the headline, skills and experience are missing the words recruiters type. A profile that is found and a profile that is ignored often differ by an hour of work.</p><p>The guides in this section cover the parts that change whether you are found and contacted: writing a headline and About section, choosing skills, asking for recommendations, and using LinkedIn to find openings and referrals rather than only applying through it. Keep your LinkedIn and resume consistent — the same titles and dates — and check the resume with the free <a href="/tools/resume-ats-checker">ATS checker</a>.</p>$i$),
  ('government-jobs-exams', $i$<p>Government jobs in India are filled through exams run by bodies such as the SSC, UPSC, the railway recruitment boards, IBPS and the state public service commissions. Each exam has its own eligibility rules, syllabus, pattern and pay level, and all of it is set out in an official notification that is long, dense and easy to misread.</p><p>The guides in this section explain those exams in plain English — eligibility, syllabus, exam pattern and salary — taken from the official notifications. For current openings with their application dates, admit cards, answer keys and results, see <a href="/government-jobs">Government Jobs</a>, which is updated from the official sources as notices are released.</p>$i$)
) as v(slug, intro)
where c.slug = v.slug;
